{"id": "extract_shared_helper_before_deleting_legacy_module", "type": "pattern", "fact": "Extract surviving helpers before deleting a legacy module", "recommendation": "In gemini_hybrid_06_legacy_chunk_pipeline_removal, chunkContext.ts could not be removed until sanitizeForFirestore() was moved into a small shared firestoreUtils.ts used by the active pipeline. When deleting a retired subsystem, first isolate any still-live pure helper into a narrow utility module, then remove the rest of the call graph atomically. This avoids leaving half-dead modules around for a single import and makes grep/typecheck validation much cleaner.", "confidence": "medium", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "gemini_hybrid_06_legacy_chunk_pipeline_removal/iteration_01", "date": "2026-03-21"}], "tags": ["architecture-refactor"], "affectedFiles": [], "createdAt": "2026-03-21T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-03-21T00:00:00Z"}
{"id": "install_missing_tools_before_declaring_blocked", "type": "pattern", "fact": "Agents must attempt to install missing CLI tools before declaring BLOCKED or stopping work", "recommendation": "Implementation agents have bash access and can install tools (apt-get, curl, npm, pip, brew, etc.). When a required tool like gcloud, terraform, or docker is not in PATH, the agent should attempt installation before giving up. Common methods: curl installer scripts, package managers, or using REST APIs with existing auth tokens as a fallback. Only declare BLOCKED after installation attempts fail AND no workaround exists. 'Tool not installed' is almost never a valid blocker \u2014 it's a solvable prerequisite.", "confidence": "medium", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "gemini_hybrid_06_whisperx_gpu_cost_evaluation/iteration_01", "date": "2026-03-23"}], "tags": ["general"], "affectedFiles": [], "createdAt": "2026-03-23T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-03-23T00:00:00Z"}
{"id": "cloud_run_fire_and_forget_needs_gen2_and_no_cpu_throttling", "type": "pattern", "fact": "Fire-and-forget Cloud Run pipelines should use gen2 and keep CPU allocated after the HTTP response", "recommendation": "In gemini_hybrid_09_cloud_run_orchestrator_01_core/iteration_01_b, the orchestrator returned `202 Accepted` immediately and continued background work. Reliable post-response execution required deploying on Cloud Run gen2 and verifying CPU was not throttled after the response; otherwise the pipeline stalled after acknowledgement. For authenticated dispatcher-orchestrator patterns, treat `--execution-environment gen2` plus no CPU throttling as part of the deployment pattern, and verify the effective service config rather than assuming the deploy flag took effect.", "confidence": "medium", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "gemini_hybrid_09_cloud_run_orchestrator_01_core/iteration_01_b", "date": "2026-03-25"}], "tags": ["architecture-refactor"], "affectedFiles": [], "createdAt": "2026-03-25T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-03-25T00:00:00Z"}
